==English==

===Etymology===
{{named-after|en|Henry Shrapnel|wplink==|nat=British|occ=army officer|born=1761|died=1842}} who invented an anti-personnel shell that transported a large number of bullets to the target before releasing them, at a far greater distance than rifles could fire the bullets individually. The surname is likely a {{glossary|metathesis|metathesized}} form of {{m|en|Charbonnel}}, a diminutive of Old French {{m|fro|charbon||charcoal}} in reference to hair color, complexion, or the like.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʃɹæpnəl/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-shrapnel.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|military|historical}} An [[anti-personnel]] [[artillery]] [[shell]] used in [[World War I]] which carries a large number of individual [[bullet]]s close to the target and then [[eject]]s them to allow them to continue along the shell's [[trajectory]] and strike the target individually.
# {{lb|en|military|historical}} The bullets from the aforementioned type of artillery shell.
# {{lb|en|military}} [[shot|Shot]], [[fragment]]s, or [[debris]] thrown out by an [[exploding]] [[shell]], bomb or [[landmine]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[loose change|Loose change]].
#* {{quote-song
|en
|lyricist=Mike Skinner
|title=Fit But You Know It
|album=A Grand Don’t Come For Free
|artist=The Streets
|year=2004
|passage=I was waiting in the queue, looking at the board / Wondering whether to have a burger or chips / Or what the '''shrapnel''' in my back pocket could afford}}
# [[debris|Debris]].
#: {{ux|en|The dog ate my sandwich, and there was '''shrapnel''' all over the place from him tearing open the bag.}}

